# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-cad-workdir-not-in-trustedpaths — a CAD with SECURELOAD=2 would refuse alfe's own runtime
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: alfe-backend
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-09-25

* Observed

Found while auditing alfe's CAD-side AutoLISP under each dialect
(clautolisp hosting the emitted run-common.lsp, 2026-09-25). The engine
reports, for every session:

#+begin_example
[sec-untrusted-load] LOAD: …/runtime/autolisp-bootstrap.lsp is a gated file
  in an untrusted location (SECURELOAD=1); on a host with SECURELOAD=2 this
  would be blocked. Add its folder to TRUSTEDPATHS to trust it.
[sec-untrusted-load] LOAD: …/runtime/autolisp-remote-io.lsp  (same)
[sec-untrusted-open] OPEN: …/protocol/alfe-eval.lsp           (same)
#+end_example

* Why it matters

alfe stages its runtime into a temporary workdir and, for every =-x=
request, writes the form to =protocol/alfe-eval.lsp= and calls the
host's native LOAD. None of that is inside TRUSTEDPATHS. On a CAD
configured with =SECURELOAD= = 2 -- "loads executable files ONLY if
their location is in TRUSTEDPATHS" (see issues/open/note-secureload.txt
and the AutoCAD documentation it cites) -- every one of those loads is
REFUSED, so alfe cannot bootstrap at all, and even a trusted runtime
directory would not save the per-request eval file.

SECURELOAD defaults to 1 on the machines we test (warn and proceed),
which is why this has never been seen on a real CAD. A hardened site --
which is exactly where a CAD-automation tool gets deployed -- would hit
it immediately, and the failure mode would be a bootstrap that never
reaches READY.

* pjb, 2026-09-25: the invocations set TRUSTEDPATHS

"Set the environment variable TRUSTEDPATHS to deal with this secureload
warning (in the uses of the tool)."

clautolisp seeds the TRUSTEDPATHS sysvar from the environment variable
of the same name under EVERY dialect
(autolisp-builtins-core/source/secureload.lisp, the SECURELOAD /
TRUSTEDPATHS pair is exempt from the clautolisp-only gate), so an
invocation declares alfe's staged runtime trusted by exporting it. The
clautolisp-hosted test harness now does exactly that before starting an
engine (TRUSTEDPATHS-SPEC / SET-ENVIRONMENT-VARIABLE in
file-protocol-tests.lisp, naming the workdir and its runtime/ and
protocol/ subdirectories), and the dialect gate consequently refuses
ANY bracketed diagnostic instead of exempting the [sec-…] family.

What remains open is the REAL CAD, where TRUSTEDPATHS is a sysvar read
from the profile/registry and no environment variable reaches it.

* Directions

- alfe knows its workdir before it launches the CAD: it can append the
  workdir (and its =protocol/= and =runtime/= subdirectories) to
  TRUSTEDPATHS in the engine's own session, restoring the previous value
  on exit -- the pattern already used for SRCHPATH
  (=_autolisp_old_srchpath= in the emitted run-common.lsp).
- Setting TRUSTEDPATHS may itself be gated on some hosts; check whether
  it can be set from LISP at all under SECURELOAD=2, or whether the
  first load has to come from an already-trusted location. If it cannot,
  the fallback is to stage the runtime under a directory the user has
  trusted once (a documented =--runtime-dir= or the installed
  =share/alfe/runtime/=, which is a stable location a site CAN trust)
  rather than a fresh temp directory per run.
- Whatever the mechanism, it must be documented: a site administrator
  has to know which directory to trust.

* Verification

The clautolisp-hosted harness added with
alfe-autocad-shadow-path-spurious-newlines reproduces the diagnostics
without a CAD (they are the =[sec-…]= lines that
=dialect-warning-lines= deliberately filters out of the dialect gate).
A real check needs a CAD with SECURELOAD set to 2 -- a Windows-runner
job.
